Differential Scanning Calorimetry. Thermal analysis was performed on a TA Instruments Q200 differential scanning calorimeter equipped with a refrigerated cooling system (RCS40). Through the sample cell, $15\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in $\Nu_{2}$ or $\Nu_{2}$ was flowed over TZero aluminum pans that were not hermetically sealed. An empty, aluminum T-Zero pan provided the reference sample for thermal analysis. The calorimeter was calibrated with the TA Instruments software package; the melting point of indium $\cdot156.60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ was utilized for the temperature calibration. Sample and pan masses were determined after activation at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $60\ \mathrm{min}$ on a TA Instruments TGA Q5000 via the readout from the internal balance under flowing $\Nu_{2}$ . Identical aliquots of mmen-2 were utilized for TGA and DSC measurements to approximate heats of adsorption. Integrated heats were calculated with TA Instruments Universal Analysis software suite. Other Physical Measurements. Elemental analyses for C, H, and N were performed at the Elemental Analysis Service Center of Sogang University or the Microanalytical Laboratory of the University of California, Berkeley. $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ spectra were obtained using a 300 MHz Varian instrument. Infrared spectra were obtained from KBr pellets with a Bomen MB-104 spectrometer or on a Perkin-Elmer Spectrum 400 FTIR spectrometer equipped with an attenuated total reflectance (ATR) accessory. For diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectroscopy (DRIFTS) spectra, the Perkin-Elmer spectrometer was equipped a Harrick Praying Mantis Diffuse Reflectance accessory and a temperature-controlled high-pressure gas cell with Swagelok valves connecting $5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in He (Praxair certified standard HE CD5C-K) and an oil-free vacuum.
## 3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Synthesis, Structure, and Activation. Reaction of $\mathrm{H}_{4}$ dobpdc with $\mathrm{ZnBr}_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or $\mathrm{MgBr}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in 1:1 DEF:EtOH afforded DEF-1 and DEF-2, respectively (see Figure 1). The coordination environment of the divalent metal cations within 1 and 2 are analogous to those in the $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobdc) series.29 In the crystal structure of DEF-1, four different dobpdc4− ligands and one DEF molecule are bonded to each $\mathrm{Zn}^{2\mp}$ ion in a distorted octahedral geometry. There are three unique O donor types from the dobpdc $^{4-}$ ligand: bridging $\left(\mu_{2}\right)$ aryloxide O atoms (O1), bridging $\left(\mu_{2}\right)$ carboxylate $\mathrm{~O~}$ atoms (O2), and nonbridging carboxylate $\mathrm{~o~}$ atoms (O3). The equatorial plane of each $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ is composed of two transdisposed O1 ligands from different linkers, one O3 donor atom, and one O2 donor atom. An O2 donor atom occupies one axial coordination site, while the other axial site is occupied by an O donor atom from DEF, the reaction solvent. This coordination mode results in the formation of helical chains of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ atoms running along the $\boldsymbol{c}$ axis of the crystal. The resulting framework consists of a honeycomb lattice of hexagonal, one-dimensional channels approximately $18.4\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ in width. Bound DEF molecules occupy the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ coordination sites along the corners of hexagonal channel walls. As shown in Figure S1, powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) data indicate DEF-2 to be isostructural with DEF-1.
Heating DEF-2 at $420^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $65\mathrm{min}$ under dynamic vacuum, removed the DEF molecules bound to the metal atoms, completely activating the material and generating open $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ coordination sites. Such extreme thermal treatment was necessary because soaking in methanol at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ did not lead to exchange of the bound DEF molecules. The porosity of activated 2 was confirmed via $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption at $77\mathrm{K}$ (see Figure S4), resulting in a BET surface area of $3270~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ . Note that, in line with the expanded structure, this is significantly greater than the BET surface area of $1495~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ reported for Mg2(dobdc).36
The synthesis and structure of mmen-2 is depicted schematically at the right of Figure 1. An activated sample of 2 was suspended in hexanes and an excess of mmen was added. As shown by powder X-ray diffraction (see Figure S3), framework crystallinity was not significantly affected by activation or subsequent amine functionalization. A much reduced BET surface area of $70~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ was calculated for mmen2, while DFT pore size distributions indicated a reduction in average pore size (see Figures S5−S7).
Upon exposure of DEF-2 to atmospheric air, the white powder turns blue and a loss of crystallinity occurs, as observed via powder X-ray diffraction measurements. Amine functionalization, however, appears to enhance framework stability, because no similar degradation was observed for mmen-2 upon exposure to air for one week.
${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ Adsorption Isotherms. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption data obtained for 2 are depicted in Figure S8. Isosteric heats of adsorption were calculated to approach $-44\mathrm{~kJ/mol}$ at low coverage, as shown in Figure S9. This value is similar to those previously reported for the analogous $\mathrm{{Mg}}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ framework.37 The adsorption capacity of 2 at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is $4.85\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ $(17.6~\mathrm{wt}~\%)$ ) and $6.42\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ $(22.0\ \mathrm{wt}\ \%)$ at 0.15 and 1 bar, respectively. The gravimetric capacity of 2 for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at 0.15 bar exceeds the capacity of all metal−organic frameworks except for $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big),^{10\mathrm{c}}$ which adsorbs $6.1\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ $21.2~\mathrm{wt}~\%)$ ) at 25 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 0.15 bar.36
The alkylamine-functionalized metal−organic framework mmen-2 displayed an extremely high affinity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at extraordinarily low pressures. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms obtained at 25, 50, and $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are presented in Figure 2. At 25 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 0.39 mbar, near the current partial pressure of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in Earth’s atmosphere, the compound adsorbed $2.0\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ (8.1 wt $\%$ ), which is 15 times the capacity of 2. At the much higher pressure of 5 mbar, the median partial pressure of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ within the International Space Station, the framework adsorbed 2.6 $\mathrm{{mmol/g}}$ $(10.3~\mathrm{\wt}~\mathrm{\%})$ . For comparison, zeolite ${\mathfrak{s h}},$ which is currently used aboard the station to adsorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ adsorbs 0.85 $\mathrm{mmol/g}$ (3.6 wt $\%_{j}$ crystallographic volumetric capacity 1.3 mmol/cm3) at 5 mbar.12,38
At ${25}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption in mmen-2 reaches 3.13 $\mathrm{mmol/g}$ (12.1 wt $\%$ ) at 0.15 bar and $3.86\mathrm{mmol/g}$ $(14.5\mathrm{wt}\ \%)$ ) at 1 bar. Remarkably, its $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake at 1 bar and $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ exceeds the amount of $\Nu_{2}$ adsorbed at $77\mathrm{K}.$ Thus, the low surface area measured at 77 K does not appear to accurately reflect the surface area accessible to $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and the pore size distribution calculated from $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption at cryogenic temperatures likely does not accurately represent the true pore size distribution within mmen-2. On a per mass basis, the amine-functionalized framework adsorbed less $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ than 2 at pressures higher than 20 mbar. The large density difference between the two frameworks is primarily responsible for the lower gravimetric capacity of mmen-2. Crystallographic densities of 0.58 and 0.86 $\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ were calculated for 2 and mmen-2, respectively. At 0.15 bar, 2 and mmen-2 adsorb 2.8 and $2.7~\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ , respectively.
Based upon the calculated number of dangling amine groups in mmen-2, a loading of $3.4\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ would correspond to one $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per amine, yet uptake of only ca. $2.2\ \mathrm{\mmol/g}$ was observed. Here, pore blockages, hydrogen bonded amines, or cooperative binding mechanisms between two amines and one $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ may be limiting the accessible stoichiometry of mmen-2. Thus, significant additional capacity improvements might be realized in the material if conditions can be identified for appending one mmen per magnesium and binding one $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule per dangling amine.
Isosteric heat of adsorption calculations were hindered by the presence of a prominent step in the isotherms at low pressures and convex to the pressure axis. Generally, continuous mathematical functions are used to model experimental isotherms, which then become the input parameters for the Clausius−Clapeyron relation. Since we were unable to mathematically model the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isotherms of mmen-2 with continuous equations over the entire pressure range, each isotherm was modeled with two Langmuir−Freundlich equations. Data sets corresponding to the adsorption regions before and after the steps were compiled and then modeled individually.
As shown in Figure 3, isosteric heats of adsorption for mmen-2 were calculated from the 25, 50, and $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ isotherm models. At low loadings, heats significantly lower than those expected for chemical adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ onto an amine were calculated. However, calculated heats quickly approached and maintained a value of $-71\mathrm{~kJ}/\mathrm{mol}.$ , which likely corresponds to the chemical adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ onto the free amine of mmen. Here, a carbamate with a weak $\mathrm{C-N}$ bond is probably formed through interaction the lone pair of the free amine of mmen and the electrophilic carbon of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
In situ diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectroscopy (DRIFTS) was employed to probe the chemical nature of adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . At $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ 1 bar of $5\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in He was introduced into an airtight gas cell containing a sample of activated mmen-2. The difference spectrum between mmen-2 under a 1 bar atmosphere and the activated framework under vacuum is shown in Figure 4. The prominent loss peak at 3316 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ , assigned to the NH stretch of free mmen-2, is indicative of chemical adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ onto amines.
Recent work on alkylamine-grafted silica surfaces have suggested that chemical adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ onto alkylamines is not possible without neighboring amines or surface hydroxyl groups to stabilize the resulting carbamates; ammonium carbamates or surface-bonded carbamates are formed, respectively.39 The formation of surface-bonded carbamates in mmen-2 is unlikely due to a lack of surface hydroxyl groups, and the broad NH stretches expected for ammonium cations are not definitively resolvable from the DRIFTS difference spectrum. Furthermore, the slow reversibility of mmen-2 at room temperature (see Figure S15) appears to preclude the formation of ammonium carbamates, which have been reported to desorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from primary amines readily at room temperature.39 Additional experiments are necessary to ascertain whether adjacent functional groups are necessary for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption within such alkylamine-functionalized metal− organic frameworks.
The step in each isotherm marks the pressure at which $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption becomes dominated by chemisorption. Interestingly, the step moves to significantly higher pressures as the adsorption temperature increases. As shown in Figure S16, the location of the step at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was reproduced four times with little variation. Thus, the step cannot be attributed to slow kinetics, but appears to be an inherent characteristic of mmen-2 $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms. All batches of mmen-2 have exhibited steps at approximately the same pressures, even when functionalized with different amounts of amines. Figure S17 presents the $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm of $\mathrm{Mg_{2}(\bar{d o b p d c})(m m e n)_{1.75}(H_{2}O)_{0.25}}$ .
The location of the step is modeled well by a simple Clausius−Clapeyron relation (see eq 4), which predicts how isotherms move as a function of temperature. In Figure 5, experimental isotherms at 25, 50, 75, 100, and $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are shown as blue, green, red, purple, and orange squares, respectively. The black lines through the 25, 50, 100, and 120 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ data are predicted isotherms for those temperatures modeled from the experimental data collected at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ by assuming that the heat of adsorption is $-71\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ for all $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ loadings. The black lines are not continuous functions, but connect the calculated points listed in Table S5. Utilizing the approximate heat of adsorption calculated from the 25, 50, and $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ isotherms, the model was able to predict the general shapes of the 100 and $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ isotherms, as well as the pressures at which the steps arise. Thus, it seems that thermodynamics alone account for the movement of the step to higher pressures at higher temperatures.
The existence of the step, however, is unexpected in a strongly adsorbing material with large pores. The amines, as the strongest adsorption sites, should preferentially adsorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ before other, weaker adsorption sites adsorb significant quantities of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . To explain the nonclassical adsorption behavior of mmen-2, we presently hypothesize that adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ onto mmen is disfavored at low adsorptive concentrations (the density of gas phase $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the pores) because of the large positive entropy associated with reorganization of the amines, as required to form a chemical bond with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Before the chemical potential necessary for amine reorganization afforded to the system by the adsorptive is achieved, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption is dominated by non-amine or weak amine- $\mathbf{\cdotCO}_{2}$ adsorption sites. The minimum chemical potential required for amine reorganization increases as thermal motion increases, in line with the shift of the step to higher pressures at higher temperatures. Additional experiments and modeling will be required to test this hypothesis and to understand the relationship between pore filling and adsorption in mmen-2. However, the shift of the step to higher pressures at higher temperatures could afford the opportunity for unique regeneration conditions, whereby a weak vacuum could nearly fully regenerate the material at moderate temperatures.
${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ Selectivity. Adsorption isotherms for ${\bf N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{~O}_{2}$ in mmen-2 at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are shown in Figure S18. The uptake capacities for these gases relative to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ suggest that it would be a highly selective adsorbent. Since we were unable to mathematically model the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isotherms of mmen-2 with a meaningful, single equation40 over the entire pressure range of interest, ideal adsorbed solution theory $(\mathrm{IA}\dot{\mathrm{ST}})^{41}$ selectivities could not be calculated. Table 1 therefore instead presents the approximate molar selectivities of mmen-2 for relevant gas mixtures, as calculated according to eq 5 above using the excess adsorption capacities. This method for determining selectivities was recently shown to be less accurate than IAST, in part because it generally underestimates the values.37
The selectivity of mmen-2 for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ in air is thus estimated to be at least $49000$ . However, the purity of gas adsorbed is perhaps a more physically meaningful value than selectivity, and is an important criterion for evaluating adsorbents if captured $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is to be transported or sequestered. The purity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed onto mmen-2, calculated according to eq $\begin{array}{r}{6,}\end{array}$ would likely be more than $96\%$ or $98\%$ for capture from air or flue gas, respectively.
While the purity of gas adsorbed within individual mmen-2 crystallites is very high, as shown in Table 1, the purity of gas adsorbed within a bed of adsorbent will be affected by the composition of gas phase molecules that fill the pore and intercrystalline spaces. For carbon capture applications, the high concentration of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ in these pore volumes will negatively affect the selectivity and purity values of real adsorbent beds. However, the approximate selectivity and purity values calculated in Table S6 indicate that the while the purity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ decreases within increasing void space, any adsorbent bed with less than $80\%$ void space should be capable of achieving $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ purity in excess of $90\%$ . From these calculations, we believe mmen-2 to be a promising, high-capacity adsorbent for removing high-purity $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from dry gas mixtures containing $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ .
$\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ Capture from Air. To evaluate the performance of mmen-2 as a regenerable adsorbent, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was utilized to monitor sample mass under dynamic environments. The top panel of Figure 6 plots changes in sample mass (normalized to the mass of the framework under ${\bf N}_{2}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) while simulated air containing $390\ \mathrm{ppm}\ \mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was flowed over the sample. Despite the very low concentration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ a $4.6\%_{\mathrm{mc}}$ $\mathrm{\Delta}\varphi_{\mathrm{{o}_{\mathrm{{mc}}}}}=$ percent mass change; $^{42}\ 1.05\ \mathrm{mmol/g};$ 4.4 wt $\%$ ) was realized after $60~\mathrm{min}$ . The adsorbent was then regenerated under flowing $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ and the cycle repeated 10 times with no apparent loss of capacity.
To the best of our knowledge, no studies evaluating the efficacy of air capture within metal−organic frameworks have yet been reported. The equilibrium capacity $\left(2.0\mathrm{mmol/g},1.72\right.$ $\mathrm{\dot{m}m o l/c m}^{3\dot{\upnu}},$ of mmen-2 is similar to the capacities of the very best amine-grafted silica and alumina adsorbents reported to date. However, the kinetics of adsorption appear to be significantly faster in mmen-2 than for amines deposited via evaporation or polymerization methodologies. For example, while the pseudo-equilibrium capacity of an outstanding poly(ethyleneimine) impregnated silica gel was reported to be ca. $2.4{\mathrm{~mmol/g}},$ it took nearly $200~\mathrm{{min}}$ for the silica based adsorbent to realize $4.6\%_{\mathrm{mc}},$ the capacity of mmen-2 for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ after only $60\ \mathrm{min}.^{23\mathrm{d}}$ The easily accessed amines within mmen2 appear to enhance adsorption rates greatly, enabling rapid adsorption−desorption cycles to be utilized.
${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ Capture from Dry Flue Gas. The capabilities of mmen-2 as an adsorbent for removing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from the flue gas of coal-fired power stations were also investigated. The bottom panel of Figure 6 presents the dynamic cycling behavior of mmen-2 under the relevant, dry conditions: $15\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ flowing over the solid at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After adsorbing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ for 15 min, the sample was heated at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $15~\mathrm{min}$ under $\Nu_{2}$ . A capacity of $11.1\%_{\mathrm{mc}}$ $\left(2.52\ \mathrm{mmol/g},\right.$ 9.9 wt $\%$ ) relative to the sample mass of mmen-2 under ${\bf N}_{2}$ at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was realized. After 50 cycles, only a $0.2\%_{\mathrm{mc}}$ capacity loss was observed. Longer adsorption and desorption times did not significantly improve the cycling capacity of the material, nor did higher desorption temperatures. Note that the apparent capacity of mmen-2 greatly exceeds the ca. 2 wt $\%$ working capacity43 of aqueous monoethanolamine (MEA) scrubbers, which would likely swing between the same adsorption and desorption temperatures.
Differential Scanning Calorimetry. To approximate the adsorbent regeneration energy, heats of adsorption and desorption were quantified for mmen-2 via differential scanning calorimetry (see Figure 8).44 When $15\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{{C}}$ was introduced over a sample of mmen-2 activated at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under ${\bf N}_{2},$ an exothermic heat flow of $-153\mathrm{J/g}$ was observed (event A). Note that, to enable rapid gas exchange, the pan was not hermetically sealed and the true value of heats evolved are likely slightly larger than those registered by the instrument due to heat loss to the environment. When this heat flow was correlated to the $2.5\ \mathrm{\mmol/g}$ capacity calculated via TGA adsorption under similar conditions, an approximate, average heat of adsorption of $-61\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was calculated.
Following a $15~\mathrm{\min}$ adsorption period, $100\%$ ${\bf N}_{2}$ was introduced into the cell and the sample was heated to $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (event B); $260\ \mathrm{J/g}$ were required to heat the sample and maintain the regeneration temperature for $15\mathrm{min}$ . Lastly, $-107$ $\mathrm{J/g}$ were evolved as the material was cooled under a $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere (event C) from 120 to $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Based upon this laboratory-scale experiment, approximately $2.34~\mathrm{MJ}$ of energy would be required to regenerate $1~\mathrm{kg}$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed onto mmen-2. This value compares very favorably with the $3.6{-4.5}$ MJ of energy necessary to release $1~\mathrm{kg}$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from an MEA scrubber.5,43
However, the aforementioned regeneration energies were measured under a $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ purge, a laboratory-scale analogue for a temperature-vacuum swing regeneration cycle. While a direct measurement of regeneration energy with a $100\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ purge was not possible, calculations shown in the SI indicate that the regeneration energy of mmen-2 under $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ will be higher primarily due to the reduced working capacity of the adsorbent. The regeneration energy of mmen-2 under $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is still lower than the regeneration energy of the most highly optimized MEA solutions, and it is likely that optimization of adsorption and desorption temperatures will further lower the heat of regeneration of mmen-2.
Approximate heat capacities $\left(C_{P}\right)$ were calculated for mmen2. Over the $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ temperature range, the average heat required to regenerate $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -loaded mmen-2 was $3.3\ \J/{\bf g}\cdot\mathrm{K}.$ The regenerated framework released an average of $1.3\mathrm{~J}/\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{K}$ upon cooling, a reasonable approximation of the heat capacity of mmen-2. For comparison, the heat capacity of a 30 wt $\%$ MEA solution without $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is ${\sim}3.8\ \mathrm{J/g{\cdot}K}$ over the same $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ range.43 The lower regeneration energy of mmen-2 is thus attributable to its lower heat capacity. In addition, the significantly greater working capacity of mmen-2 means that a smaller mass is required to adsorb the same amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The heat required to desorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from the amines is similar for mmen-2 and MEA solutions.43
## 4. CONCLUSIONS AND OUTLOOK
With the new metal−organic frameworks $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ $\mathbf{\left(M=\right}$ $Z\mathrm{n}$ (1), $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ (2)), an expanded variant of the $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ (MOF-74) structure type has been achieved, leading to enlarged, 18.4-Å-wide channels lined with open metal coordination sites. Functionalization of 2 with mmen, afforded a remarkable new $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbent, mmen- $\mathbf{\cdotM}_{\mathbf{\cdot}}$ (dobpdc). The large capacity, high selectivity, and fast kinetics of this material for adsorbing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from dry gas mixtures with ${\bf N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ make it an attractive new adsorbent for applications in which zeolites and inorganic bases are currently used, including the removal of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from air.
Future work will focus on evaluating the efficacy of alkylamine-appended metal−organic frameworks such as mmen-2 under humid conditions. Whether the presence of water in gas mixtures will significantly affect the stability, capacity, selectivity, or regeneration energy of mmen-2 is presently unknown. In addition, the peculiar $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isotherm shapes warrant further investigation, since a detailed understanding of the adsorption behavior within mmen-2 may enable the synthesis of even better materials for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture. Finally, efforts are also underway to improve the separation performance of the material through use of various other polyamines in place of mmen.
